CLAIM 13 — Per-directory agent memory injected into worker prompts

Scope of inspection (static only): src/goal/cli-runner.ts, src/goal/cli-worker.ts,
src/goal/lessons.ts, src/goal/planner.ts, src/goal/plan-autocheck.ts, and the
cli-runner / cli-worker test files.

What the code actually does (narrow scope)
==========================================

A. CLAUDE.md is read from the *per-run working directory* and threaded into the
   worker prompt — but only for the Codex backend.

   - cli-runner.ts:37-38 — `const projectConventions = this.backend === "codex"
     ? readProjectConventions(context.workingDir) : undefined;` (the read path
     is gated on backend === "codex").
   - cli-runner.ts:76-84 — `readProjectConventions` reads `<workingDir>/CLAUDE.md`
     from disk for each step, returning the trimmed content or `undefined`.
   - cli-runner.ts:40-60 — `projectConventions` is forwarded to
     `executeTaskWithCliWorker` as a named field.
   - cli-worker.ts:60 — `CliWorkerParams.projectConventions?: string`.
   - cli-worker.ts:231, 267-272, 284 — `projectConventions` is passed into both
     `buildCliPromptPayload` (for persistence) and `buildCliArgs` (for the
     spawned CLI invocation).
   - cli-worker.ts:684-708 — for Codex, when `projectConventions` is non-empty,
     `buildCliPromptPayload` prepends a `## PROJECT CONVENTIONS` section to the
     prompt sent to the `codex exec` subprocess (the section is separated by
     `PROMPT_SECTION_DIVIDER`).

   Test coverage confirming the path:
   - cli-runner.test.ts:70 — "passes CLAUDE.md content to codex workers"
   - cli-runner.test.ts:72 — writes CLAUDE.md into a temp working dir and
     asserts the spawned codex args include it.
   - cli-runner.test.ts:83 — "omits project conventions for codex workers when
     CLAUDE.md is missing".
   - cli-runner.test.ts:95 — writes CLAUDE.md but asserts it is NOT injected
     for the Claude Code backend (cli-worker.test.ts:294 also asserts
     "PROJECT CONVENTIONS (from CLAUDE.md):" does not appear for Claude Code).

   Important narrowing: for the Claude Code backend the harness does NOT
   read or inject CLAUDE.md itself — it relies on Claude Code's own native
   discovery of CLAUDE.md from the spawn cwd (set to `context.workingDir`
   at cli-worker.ts:330). So per-directory CLAUDE.md is honored end to end
   for both backends, but via different mechanisms.

B. AGENTS.md is NOT read by the goal worker at runtime.

   - The goal worker never opens `<workingDir>/AGENTS.md` itself; the only
     AGENTS.md handling in the goal subsystem is:
     * conventions.ts:30 — seeds `~/.codex/AGENTS.md` once (global, not
       per-directory).
     * planner.ts:51, 54, 61 + plan-autocheck.ts:43, 569 — the planner/reviewer
       prompts mention that the *generated plan* should respect/produce
       CLAUDE.md and AGENTS.md, but no Node-side read of AGENTS.md is performed
       at worker spawn time.
   - Codex itself reads AGENTS.md natively when run with `--cd <workingDir>`
     (cli-worker.ts:790-792), so per-directory AGENTS.md still reaches Codex
     workers — but as a CLI-native feature, not because the harness injects it.

C. MEMORY.md is NOT part of the goal-worker prompt-assembly path.

   - All MEMORY.md handling lives in a different subsystem (the PI agent /
     memory manager under `src/memory/` and `src/agents/`); a recursive grep
     across src/goal/ returns zero hits for MEMORY.md. Public README wording
     must not imply that goal workers consume MEMORY.md.

D. Lessons ARE per-working-directory.

   - lessons.ts:174-180 — `getLessonsForContext(workingDir)` filters every
     stored lesson by `lesson.workingDir === workingDir` for project-scope
     lessons; global-scope lessons leak across directories by design.
   - cli-runner.ts:33-36 — the working directory's filtered lessons are
     passed into the worker.
   - cli-worker.ts:540-582 — `buildCliWorkerPrompt` emits a
     "LESSONS FROM PRIOR RUNS" block in the prompt that lists each
     {pattern, lesson} pair. Visible in this very worker's own prompt at
     the "LESSONS FROM PRIOR RUNS" section.

